WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.879
STEWART BRAND: I sort of know you through Brian Eno, an old friend who's been associated

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:11.730
with Long Now for a long time. And your
collaboration with him has been amazing

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.139
in terms of the product. But you're
collaborative generally; you seem very

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:21.840
comfortable working with other artists,
and out of the interplay getting

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.460
some extra thing. Say more
about how that works for you.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:36.570
DAVID BYRNE: For me, when a collaboration works—and it
it can work fairly often, pretty

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.190
good percentage of times—you get
something extra. You get something that

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.840
neither of the collaborators would have
done on their own. And both of them,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.789
or however many there are, realize, "Oh,
this is greater than the sum of its

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:56.070
parts." Or it's a third thing that has
come into existence that is neither of

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:02.129
us, but it's something else.
It doesn't always work. There's

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:08.909
some times when it just doesn't. But when
it does, that's really exciting. I've

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:17.070
heard other musicians say that
they hate the idea of collaborations.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:24.750
To some, it feels to them that it
is watering down the authenticity of

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:32.150
the author. It's a compromise of some
sort—which it is. You're inevitably

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:38.119
compromising, but if you get something
greater, to me that's worth it.

